welcome back to cloud storage bytes now  that you've got the billing basics let's  add a level of complexity like when a  client needs to be billed for their  usage of your resources today on cloud  storage bytes will take you into the  world of requestor pays  whenever a user accesses a cloud storage  resource like a bucket or an object  there are charges associated with both  making and executing the request this  includes operation charges for  performing a request Network charges for  reading the data and data retrieval  charges if the data is stored as  Nearline storage or cold line storage  now normally the project owner of the  resource is billed for these access  charges but this isn't always  appropriate  if the requester provides a billing  project with their request the  requesters project can be billed instead  and this feature is appropriately called  requester pays with requester pays  enabled on your bucket you can require  requesters to include a billing project  in their request  thus billing the requesters project  enabling requester pays is useful for  example if you have a lot of data that  you want to make available to your users  but you don't want to be charged for  their access of that data note that  there are some charges that are always  going to be applied to the project  containing the bucket even when  requester pays is enabled these charges  include storage charges for storing data  in a bucket and any applicable early  deletion charges for complete details on  those caveats go ahead and check out the  docs linked below  and now let's look into how to enable  requester pays start in the cloud  storage browser in the Google cloud  platform console in the list of buckets  find the bucket that you want to enable  and click the off button in the  requester pays column in the window that  appears click enable once enabled a  green bubble and on will appear in the  requester pays column for that bucket of  course once you enable requester pays  you may need to disable it so let's walk  through that process as well to disable  requester pays on a bucket go ahead and  start in the cloud storage browser in  the Google cloud platform console in the  list of buckets find the bucket you want  to disable and click the on button in  the requester pays column in the window  that appears click disable once disabled  a gray bubble and off will appear in  there  mr. paise column to check whether  requestor pays is enabled on a bucket  take a look at the list of buckets the  requester pays status of each bucket is  found in that requester pays column so  we've looked at how to enable and  disable requester pays on your own  storage buckets but what if you're on  the other side what if you're the  account who wants to access an object  stored in a requester pays bucket let's  checkout accessing requester pays  buckets using GS util here's how to  include a billing projects that you can  download an object store it in a  requester pays bucket project ID is the  ID of the project to be billed for  example my - project bucket name is the  name of the relevant bucket for example  my - bucket object name is the name of  the object you want to download for  example pets / dog object destination is  the location where you want to save your  object for example desktop / dog keep in  mind that buckets that have requester  pays disabled still accept requests that  include a billing project and charges  are applied to the building project  supplied in the request so make sure you  consider any billing implications prior  to including a billing project in any of  your requests there's more to come about  cloud storage but that's gonna be for  another episode if you want to know more  about using requester pays check out the  links in the description don't forget to  subscribe give us a like and tell us  what you want to learn about Google  Cloud storage thanks for joining us for  this quick byte of cloud storage see you  next time  [Music]
 

